  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nFocus of discussion and any forecast updates will be on next 24 to \n36 hours with regards to severe and hydrologic concerns. Getting \ndiscussion out early to to potential storms over next couple of \nhours. \n\nWe are monitoring a cluster of thunderstorms from SE WI into \nnorthern IL. While some occasional severe reports came from earlier \nstorms, the main storm of interest (HP Supercell) is rooted on the \noutflow boundary from the earlier storms with some hints at this \nmoving more SE with time. Other storms continue to try to develop \nand expand, including far NW parts of the area. This leaves a lot of \nuncertainty as to what occurs overnight. Plenty of instability \nremains across the area, but near sfc inhibition may limit wind \npotential overnight. Suffice to say, risk of severe remains in play \nacross the area. For the latest updates on the mesoscale features, \nmonitor SPC products. In addition, a Flash Flood Watch was issued \nearlier to cover several counties to cover the recent rainfall and \npotential for some areas seeing more heavy rain overnight. This is \ncurrently in effect until 11 am EDT, but given the concerns of the \nconvective evolution overnight no changes are planned at this time. \n\nIn the wake of the overnight/early AM storms, the atmosphere will \nrecharge once again with plenty of CAPE and sufficient shear to bring \nyet another severe threat Monday afternoon and evening. Much like \ntoday, the main threat will be damaging wind along with some large \nhail. If any outflow boundaries linger in the area, these could help \nwith a localized tornado threat. Also in play will be the continued \nthreat of heavy rainfall as PWATs remain about 1.75\" and warm cloud \ndepths of 14 K to 15kft. May need to expand the flood watch in area \nand time depending on what evolves overnight. Sadly, trends in the \nmodels do show yet more storms developing later Monday evening into \nTuesday morning (possibly the 4th round of storms since Sunday \nevening). Not even going to try to do anything in the forecast with \nthis given the plethora of challenges from previous rounds of storms \nthat cannot be fully resolved this far out. \n\nDeepening on the location of the stationary boundary Tuesday, one \nmore round of convection could occur focused south of US-30. Again, \nno changes to forecast with regards to this, just something to keep \nin mind with the flood threat possibly still there. \n\nBeyond Tuesday night, models seem to push the effective boundary \nsouth of the area to give us a reprieve from the rain and storms. \nHowever, the overall upper level flow doesn't change much so low \nconfidence at this point and model blend will be left alone.",
